1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Today, we will build a simple introduction page that opens in a web browser. By the end, you will know how to create a file, a heading, a paragraph, a list, and a link.

2
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:36,000
HTML is a markup language. It tells a browser what each piece of content is and how the page is structured. The browser reads the file and displays it as a web page.

3
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:54,000
First, create a folder and add a file named index dot H T M L. Open that file in Visual Studio Code or another text editor, then write your code.

4
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The doctype on the first line tells the browser to use modern HTML rules. It is document information, so visitors do not see it as page text.

5
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The lang attribute tells the browser what language the page uses. Screen readers and other tools can use this information to present the page more clearly.

6
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The head contains useful page information, such as character encoding, the mobile viewport, and the browser tab title. This information is not shown like regular page content.

7
00:01:48,000 --> 00:02:06,000
The body contains the content visitors can see: headings, text, lists, and links. Close the head, open the body, and close both elements at the end.

8
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Use an H one heading for the page's main title. Choose a clear title, and remember to write both the opening tag and its matching closing tag.

9
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Put a short introduction inside a P tag. You can describe what the page is about. For privacy, you may use a fictional name instead of your own.

10
00:02:42,000 --> 00:03:00,000
U L creates a bulleted list, and each L I tag creates one list item. Put every item in its own L I element so the browser can display the list correctly.

11
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:18,000
An A tag creates a link. Its H R E F attribute tells the browser where to go. Write link text that describes the destination, such as, view my project.

12
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Now combine the doctype, HTML, head, and body with your text, list, and link. Consistent indentation makes it easier to understand which elements are nested inside others.

13
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Save the file and open it in a browser. After you make a change, save again and reload. Check the tab title, heading, paragraph, list, and link.

14
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:12,000
If something is missing, check that each tag is closed, the spelling is correct, and the file is saved. Also check that the link points to the right file path.

15
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Your turn: add one H one, an introduction paragraph, three learning interests, and one project link. Open it in a browser and use the checklist to review your work.

16
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:48,000
You have used an HTML file, head and body, H one, P, U L and L I, and an A tag with H R E F. In the next lesson, we will organize the page with semantic elements.
